A||||6||Cooter||What is a. . . (yuck, a Ford question)||||1||
Z||000000||Cooter||10-29-2000||07:33 PM||||1969 Mercury Comet Cyclone GT/CJ? It used to have a 428 in it, but that was replaced by a late model 302. I am not a Ford person, so can someone tell me about this car please?||||reg||1
Z||000001||stimpy||10-29-2000||10:26 PM||||Wasn't that the bad-ass NASCAR thing that CHrysler bult th Superbird to beat? It's a fastback-looking car, right? And the CJ thing can only mean either a Jeep or a Cobra Jet. Why, you got one?||||reg||1
Z||000002||frontal lobe||10-29-2000||10:27 PM||||I think you may have 2 Mercury names going there, Cooter.  I think the Comet was the same as the Ford Maverick, their small car of the day.<P>I think the Cyclone was the mid-size sedan that was the equivalent of a Ford Torino.  In 1968 it would have been a Fairlane, but in 1969 Ford switched their mid-size sedans to the Torino.<P>The Mercury Cyclone with the 428 (or was it a 429?  The memory fades...) was sort of the FoMoCo muscle car answer to the Dodge Coronets and Superbees.  That car really hauled!  I don't know how it would do with a 302.<P>The fastest I've ever driven a car was my grandma's 1968 Ford Fairlane with the 351 cleveland engine in it.  120mph on highway 64 in Illinois between highway 51 (now 39) and Dekalb for you Illinoisans.  Very flat and straight.<P>Good question, Cooter.  Are you going to be asking about the Mercury Bobcat next!?!?<P>Hey, if you are going to get a Cyclone, I may have to bug you for a ride in it for old times sake.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Old Lar||10-29-2000||10:31 PM||||69 was a long time ago, but if I recall  the Couger was Mercury's pricier version of the Mustang, the Comet was small to midsize.  The Cyclone GT was the response to the Boss Mustang that had a big engine, long hood and a large windowed fast back with sunshade louvers, so the car wasn't as much a greenhouse.  Someone probably beat the crap out of the 428 and sawpped in the small Foed engine.  Ford used to have the Torino with the big block too.  All those fun cars that got 6 mpg and when gas was $0.30 a gallon.<p>[This message has been edited by Old Lar (edited 10-29-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||Cooter||10-29-2000||11:06 PM||||my dad picked up one today. It is a complete car- we drove it home. It seems pretty solid, little rough around the edges, but fairly straight. It is an automatic on the column with a bench seat, factory tach etc. I have never seen one before, so any info would be greatly appreciated. ||||reg||1
Z||000005||frontal lobe||10-30-2000||03:08 PM||||Cooter,<P>Check out this website:  <A HREF="http://users.nowonline.net/cyclone/" TARGET=_blank>http://users.nowonline.net/cyclone/</A> <P>Pretty interesting reading.  I was only 13 when the car came out, so forgive the misinformation above.<P>Now, about getting that ride in it...||||reg||1
Z||000006||pollock||11-01-2000||02:28 PM||||Do a search on a guy named Kevin Marti, or go to <A HREF="http://www.vintage-mustang.com." TARGET=_blank>www.vintage-mustang.com.</A> <P>Marti has published books on old ford data, and has apparently been handed the responsibility by Ford.  <P>If anybody knows, he will.||||reg||1
